7. Update the database for the new relationship
Because you already created the database on Day 12, adding user_id to your Python model will not automatically change the existing PostgreSQL table.
You have two choices:
•	Learning project with no important data: Back up anything you need, drop and recreate ai_platform_db, then let SQLAlchemy create the tables.
•	Preserve existing records: Use a database migration or run the following SQL in pgAdmin Query Tool.
For the second option, execute:
</>SQL
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(150) UNIQUE NOT NULL
);

ALTER TABLE ai_requests
ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS user_id INTEGER
REFERENCES users(id);

CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ix_ai_requests_user_id
ON ai_requests(user_id);

This creates the users table and adds the foreign key column without deleting your existing request records.
For a production application, use Alembic migrations rather than manually modifying database tables.
